In scripts/core/updates.py, make _version_key pre-release-aware by splitting off the SemVer pre-release tail BEFORE the existing chunk tokenization, and ranking "no pre-release" above "has pre-release":

    def _version_key(v: str) -> tuple:
        core, sep, pre = v.partition("-")
        core_parts = _tokenize(core)
        if not sep:
            # No pre-release: sort AFTER any pre-release of the same
            # core (1.0.0 > 1.0.0-rc1). Sentinel (1,) > (0, ...).
            return (core_parts, (1,))
        return (core_parts, (0, _tokenize(pre)))

    def _tokenize(s: str) -> tuple:
        parts: list[tuple] = []
        for chunk in re.split(r"[^A-Za-z0-9]+", s):
            if not chunk:
                continue
            for m in _VERSION_PART_RE.finditer(chunk):
                num, alpha = m.group(1), m.group(2)
                if num is not None:
                    parts.append((0, int(num)))
                else:
                    parts.append((1, alpha.lower()))
        return tuple(parts)

This keeps the existing numeric/alpha tokenization (so "1.10.0" > "1.9.0", "v1.0.0" != "1.0.0", and "1.0a" handling are unchanged), only adding the pre-release split. Note: partition on the FIRST '-' is the right SemVer boundary; do not split on '.'/'_' for the pre-release demarcation. Two no-prerelease versions still compare by core then equal sentinel, so existing behavior is preserved. Then (a) correct the now-wrong inline comment (it implies "1.0" > "1.0a", which was false before and is irrelevant after), and (b) tighten the test: replace `assert result in (-1, 1)` with `assert self.mod.compare_versions("1.0.0","1.0.0-rc1") == 1` and add `assert self.mod.compare_versions("1.0.0-rc1","1.0.0-rc2") == -1` to pin pre-release ordering. Scope is desktop-update-only: no KJV/byte-stability and no marathon-core impact. Land as a local commit per the findings-only/save-cadence rule.

